<Base title="Prologue — The Sustainability Diet" description="The Prologue of The Sustainability Diet by Karen Pendergrass." readingBar>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-reading px-6 pt-20 pb-32" id="prologue">

    <header class="mb-14">
      <p class="small-caps text-xs text-ochre mb-4">From the book</p>
      <div class="small-caps text-xs text-ink/50 mb-3">Prologue</div>
      <h1 class="font-display text-4xl md:text-5xl tracking-tight leading-tight">
        One Hundred Camels
      </h1>
    </header>

    <div class="prose-reading text-lg md:text-xl leading-[1.75] text-ink/90 space-y-7">

      <p class="first-letter:font-display first-letter:text-6xl first-letter:leading-none first-letter:mr-2 first-letter:float-left first-letter:text-ochre">
        It is not lost on me that for three million years, millions of
        camels lumbered through the very place where I first sat down
        to write this book. A cafe in Santa Monica called 212. The
        cafe is gone now. The camels have been gone much longer.
      </p>

      <p>
        The camel was <em>Camelops hesternus</em>, "yesterday's camel,"
        the Western camel, native to North America for almost the
        entire existence of its genus. It stood roughly seven feet
        at the shoulder, somewhat larger than a modern dromedary,
        built for the shortgrass and sage of the late Pleistocene.
        Its bones turn up across the American West, from the La Brea
        tar pits in southern California to kill sites in the Great
        Basin to arroyos in the southern Plains. It ate mesquite and
        prickly pear. It browsed juniper. It moved in herds across
        a continent where horses, mammoths, ground sloths, American
        lions, and American cheetahs shared the same country. Then,
        about thirteen thousand years ago, it was gone.<sup><a href="#fn1" id="ref1">1</a></sup>
      </p>

      <p>
        Most readers know this story, if they know it at all, in
        reverse. The camels Americans picture are Old World animals
        &mdash; dromedaries and Bactrians, the camels of the Silk
        Road and the Sahara, introduced to the Americas once, briefly,
        by the US Army Camel Corps in the 1850s, then failed out of
        service and absorbed into folklore. The ancestors of those
        Old World camels evolved on the North American continent and
        migrated out across the Bering land bridge millions of years
        ago. They diversified in Eurasia and Africa. They returned
        to their continent of origin only as imports. The camel's
        deep home is here. We have forgotten.
      </p>

      <p>
        The forgetting is not personal. It is structural, and it is
        the reason I am writing this book.
      </p>

      <p>
        The framework most of us have inherited for deciding what
        belongs on this continent begins in 1492. Animals present
        here before Columbus are "native." Animals introduced after
        Columbus are "non-native," "invasive," "exotic" &mdash; the
        vocabulary varies with the disposition of the speaker, but
        the baseline does not. The line is drawn at the date of
        European contact. What was here at that moment became the
        reference point against which all subsequent ecological change
        is measured.
      </p>

      <p>
        It is an oddly narrow window. Camels had been absent from
        North America for roughly twelve and a half thousand years
        by the time Columbus made landfall. Horses had been absent
        for about the same span. Mammoths, mastodons, ground sloths,
        American cheetahs, American lions, giant beavers, dire wolves,
        and most of the continent's megafaunal assemblage were already
        gone. The continent the Spanish sailed into was not a pristine
        ecosystem frozen at its baseline. It was a depleted one, long
        depleted, more than half of its large-bodied fauna already
        missing. The Columbian reference date captures the moment of
        European contact. It does not capture anything ecological
        about the land itself.
      </p>

      <p>
        Paul S. Martin spent most of his career at the University of
        Arizona trying to put this observation into wider circulation.
        Martin was a paleoecologist who had worked on the Late
        Pleistocene extinctions for decades, and by the time he
        published <em>Twilight of the Mammoths</em> in 2005 he had
        arrived at a position that is still, twenty years later,
        outside most popular conservation conversation. He proposed
        that the question "what belongs here" should be answered on
        a deeper timescale than 1492. He called the conservation
        paradigm that stops at Columbus "the Columbian Curtain." He
        proposed dropping it.<sup><a href="#fn2" id="ref2">2</a></sup>
      </p>

      <p>
        On the other side of the Curtain is a different continent.
        Mammoths in the boreal north. Ground sloths the size of bears
        browsing the forest edges. American horses on the plains.
        American cheetahs in the canyons &mdash; which is why the
        pronghorn, the fastest land animal in North America, still
        runs at speeds no living predator requires; its legs are
        calibrated to a cat that no longer exists. And camels.
        <em>Camelops</em> across the basin and range. <em>Hemiauchenia</em>
        &mdash; a larger, stouter relative &mdash; through the woodlands.
        Smaller llamas along the southwestern edge. A continent
        arranged around a set of animal relationships that have,
        almost entirely, gone unreciprocated for a hundred and twenty
        centuries.
      </p>

      <p>
        Martin was not sentimental about this. He was a scientist,
        and he had the fossil record to work with. His argument was
        that ecological function does not care about the date on
        which a species disappeared. A prairie that evolved with
        proboscideans behaves differently in their absence than one
        that evolved without them. A predator-prey dynamic misses
        both parties when either one is removed. A fruit tree whose
        seeds evolved to be eaten by ground sloths is still waiting
        for the ground sloths. The ecosystem is a set of relationships,
        and most of the relationships on this continent in the late
        Pleistocene have been one-sided for a very long time.
      </p>

      <p>
        If you grew up in the American Midwest or the mid-South, you
        have almost certainly met an osage orange tree. <em>Maclura
        pomifera</em>. It is common along fence rows and in old hedgerow
        plantings &mdash; it was planted massively in the nineteenth
        century as a thorny living fence before barbed wire made it
        obsolete &mdash; and its fruit is the size and approximate
        color of a softball, grainy-skinned, slightly fragrant, faintly
        sticky with a white latex when cut. To any modern animal of
        the continent, that fruit is essentially inedible. Horses will
        nose one and move on. Cattle will not eat them. Squirrels pick
        through the seeds if they can reach them, which is rarely. The
        fruit falls, rots in place, and stains the ground. A tree with
        a bumper crop looks like it is producing for no one.
      </p>

      <p>
        Because, in a real sense, it is. In 1982, Daniel Janzen and
        Paul Martin published a paper in <em>Science</em> titled
        "Neotropical Anachronisms: The Fruits the Gomphotheres
        Ate."<sup><a href="#fn3" id="ref3">3</a></sup> The argument was
        straightforward, and once you have read it you cannot un-see
        it. Many of the large, sugary, otherwise-puzzling fruits of
        the Americas &mdash; avocados, pawpaws, honey locust pods,
        osage oranges, a long list of tropical species &mdash; carry
        the structural signatures of having co-evolved with large-bodied
        dispersers that are no longer there. A gomphothere, a
        mastodon-relative proboscidean that ranged widely across North
        and Central America until the end of the Pleistocene, would
        have taken an osage orange whole, walked some miles, and
        deposited the seeds in a dung pile prepared for germination.
        Without the gomphothere, the tree continues to produce the
        fruit the tree evolved to produce. The fruit is a message to
        a mouth that no longer exists.
      </p>

      <p>
        Janzen and Martin called these trees ecological anachronisms.
        The phrase stayed with me.
      </p>

      <p>
        An anachronism, strictly, is something out of its time &mdash;
        a character in the wrong century, a gesture from the wrong era.
        The osage orange is out of its time because its partner is out
        of its time. The tree is stranded in a present that has
        forgotten what it is for. Multiply this by every species with
        a large-fruit architecture, every grassland assemblage that
        evolved under the hooves and digestive tracts of animals
        weighing tons, every predator community whose prey has been
        gone for longer than there has been writing, and you have
        something like the actual ecological state of this continent.
        The landscape is not depleted in a vague or poetic way. It is
        missing specific animals. Their absence has specific
        consequences. The consequences have compounded across twelve
        thousand years of quiet rearrangement that most of us have not
        been taught to notice.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is what the Columbian Curtain hides. Drop the baseline
        back to the end of the Pleistocene and the continent you are
        standing on is not recovering from European contact; it is
        recovering from something much older, and most of that
        recovery has not yet begun.
      </p>

      <p>
        I should say that I have held various positions on this
        material over the years. I first read Martin in the late
        2000s, and my initial reading left me convinced of a simpler
        story than I now think the evidence supports. For a long time
        I believed that the extinctions had been driven primarily by
        humans arriving across the Bering land bridge &mdash; the
        "overkill hypothesis," which Martin himself had done much to
        establish in his earlier career &mdash; and that the case for
        rewilding followed from a kind of collective human debt. Later
        work on rapid climate oscillation during the Younger Dryas,
        on the paleoclimate record more broadly, and on the difficulty
        of cleanly dating the extinctions against human arrival, has
        made me less confident. The question of cause is not settled,
        and I no longer think the rewilding argument depends on its
        resolution. Chapter 3 returns to this in the detail it deserves.
        The prologue does not need to.
      </p>

      <p>
        What I want to put on the table first, before any framework
        or argument, is the shape of the absence itself. The animals
        are not here. The fruit is still being made. The grasslands
        still stand in the assemblages that evolved around grazers
        that are gone. The pronghorn still runs from a cheetah.
      </p>

      <p class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl leading-snug text-ink not-italic">
        The continent is a library that has lost most of its librarians,
        and it is somehow still trying to operate.
      </p>

      <p class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl leading-snug text-ochre italic">
        I would like, somewhere in my life, to see one hundred camels
        in an osage orange orchard.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is plainly said because there is no more elegant way to
        say it. I do not mean it as policy, or as provocation, or as
        a headline. I mean it as a wish &mdash; a specific one, with
        specific animals, in a specific arrangement. A hundred camels
        because that is enough for a small, real herd; large enough
        to be a population and small enough to manage honestly. An
        osage orange orchard because I would like to know what those
        trees look like when their fruit has a mouth. Camels are not
        gomphotheres, but they are closer in body plan, gut size, and
        browsing habit than anything now available on the continent,
        and they are already here &mdash; feral in parts of the
        American West, stabled on hobby ranches elsewhere, just not
        in the ecological role they once had. I would like to know
        whether the fruit gets eaten. I would like to know whether
        the seeds, passed through a large mammalian gut and deposited
        with fertilizer, germinate at higher rates than the current
        state of abandonment allows. I would like to know whether the
        ground around the trees begins, even slightly, to resemble
        something other than rot.
      </p>

      <p>
        I am not sure anyone would fund the experiment. I am not sure
        the permits would be approved, or the ecological risk
        assessments would come out favorably, or the neighbors would
        agree. I know what serious critics of Pleistocene rewilding
        would say about it &mdash; I have read their arguments, and
        the book takes them seriously in the chapters where they
        belong. The wish is not a program. It is the thing the program
        would be for.
      </p>

      <p>
        This book is, for the most part, an analytical argument about
        how to evaluate food systems. It proposes a framework, applies
        it, and reaches conclusions I think are defensible. But the
        analytical case is not what drives it. What drives it is this
        &mdash; the orchard, the camels, the fruit with a mouth. The
        sense that we have been living for twelve thousand years on
        a continent whose ecological vocabulary has been spoken at
        half-volume, and that there is no reason to treat that
        half-silence as a permanent condition.
      </p>

      <p>
        Paul Martin spent his career trying to make the silence
        legible. I am writing this book, more than anything else, to
        return his argument to a conversation that has mostly moved
        on without it.
      </p>

      <p class="font-display text-xl leading-snug text-ink">
        The framework comes later. The camels come first.
      </p>

    </div>

